WEBVTT

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.784
In 1995, there was a programming language
that more people used than C, more people

00:00:09.834 --> 00:00:15.619
used than C++, more people used than Java
— which had literally just been invented.

00:00:15.669 --> 00:00:21.064
It was the single most popular programming
language on the planet, and it powered

00:00:21.114 --> 00:00:25.343
everything from corporate accounting software
to shareware games your dad downloaded

00:00:25.393 --> 00:00:26.510
off a BBS.

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:28.800
That language was Visual Basic.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.552
And today, almost nobody talks about it.

00:00:31.602 --> 00:00:36.157
If you're under 30 and you learned to
program, you probably learned Python, or

00:00:36.207 --> 00:00:37.472
JavaScript, or maybe C++.

00:00:37.522 --> 00:00:41.419
You've probably never written a
line of Visual Basic in your life.

00:00:41.469 --> 00:00:44.050
You might not even know what it is.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:47.841
So I'm going to write something in it.

00:00:47.891 --> 00:00:54.105
Specifically, I'm going to build a complete
simulation of the card game War — two

00:00:54.155 --> 00:00:59.535
computer players, a full deck of 52 cards,
shuffling, dealing, comparing, and the

00:00:59.585 --> 00:01:05.799
whole "I declare war" mechanic — all in
Visual Basic, written the way someone would

00:01:05.849 --> 00:01:09.140
have actually written it in 1995 or 1996.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:15.393
And along the way, I'm going to explain
what this language was, why it mattered, why

00:01:15.443 --> 00:01:20.470
millions of people loved it, and why
Microsoft eventually put a bullet in it.

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:21.560
Let's go.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:30.215
To understand Visual Basic,
you need to understand BASIC.

00:01:30.265 --> 00:01:34.887
Capital B, capital A, capital
everything — it's an acronym.

00:01:34.937 --> 00:01:37.223
Beginner's All-purpose
Symbolic Instruction Code.

00:01:37.273 --> 00:01:44.231
It was invented in 1964 at Dartmouth
College by John Kemeny and Thomas Kurtz, and

00:01:44.281 --> 00:01:49.370
its entire reason for existing
was that programming was too hard.

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:55.474
In 1964, if you wanted to write a program,
you were probably writing FORTRAN or

00:01:55.524 --> 00:01:58.866
COBOL or assembly language. You
were probably punching cards.

00:01:58.916 --> 00:02:00.751
The learning curve was brutal.

00:02:00.801 --> 00:02:05.274
Kemeny and Kurtz wanted something that
a college freshman with no technical

00:02:05.324 --> 00:02:09.420
background could sit down and
start using. So they made BASIC.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:12.690
And it worked. It worked incredibly well.

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:17.928
Through the 1970s and 1980s, BASIC became
the language that shipped with almost

00:02:17.978 --> 00:02:23.569
every home computer. Your Commodore 64 had
BASIC. Your Apple II had Applesoft BASIC.

00:02:23.619 --> 00:02:25.181
Your TRS-80 had BASIC.

00:02:25.231 --> 00:02:31.627
When you turned on a home computer in
1982, you were usually staring at a BASIC

00:02:31.677 --> 00:02:32.030
prompt.

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:37.275
This is important because it means that
an entire generation of programmers — the

00:02:37.325 --> 00:02:42.755
people who would go on to build the software
industry — learned to code by writing

00:02:42.805 --> 00:02:43.440
things like:

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:45.469
BASIC was how you learned.

00:02:45.519 --> 00:02:49.890
It was the Python of its era, except
it came pre-installed on your hardware.

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:52.260
Now, fast-forward to 1991.

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:57.060
Microsoft has Windows 3.0, and
Windows programming is a nightmare.

00:02:57.110 --> 00:03:04.260
If you want to write a Windows application
in C, you need to understand message

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:09.540
loops, window procedures, callback functions,
resource files, the entire Win16 API.

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:16.260
A "Hello World" window in C is 73 lines
of code. It's not beginner-friendly.

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:17.700
It's barely expert-friendly.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:22.910
A guy named Alan Cooper had built a prototype
of a drag-and-drop interface builder.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:26.990
He called it "Tripod." You could
take a blank window and just...

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:31.070
drop a button onto it. Drop
a text box. Drop a label.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:34.470
And it would wire up the
event handling for you.

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:42.112
Microsoft saw this, bought it, married it
to a BASIC dialect, and in May of 1991,

00:03:42.162 --> 00:03:44.360
Visual Basic 1.0 was born.

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:46.247
And it changed everything.

00:03:46.297 --> 00:03:51.635
Here's what made Visual Basic revolutionary:
in C, if you wanted a button that said

00:03:51.685 --> 00:03:57.023
"Click Me" and popped up a message, you
were writing dozens of lines of boilerplate.

00:03:57.073 --> 00:04:02.412
In Visual Basic, you drew the button on the
form with your mouse, double-clicked it,

00:04:02.462 --> 00:04:03.130
and typed:

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:08.330
That's it. Three lines. You hit F5,
the program ran, the button worked.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:14.542
The gap between having an idea and seeing
it work on screen was shorter than it had

00:04:14.592 --> 00:04:17.100
ever been in the history of programming.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:24.131
VB 1.0 came out in 1991 for
Windows 3.0. VB 2.0 in 1992.

00:04:24.181 --> 00:04:31.725
VB 3.0 in 1993 added database support through
something called the Jet engine — yes,

00:04:31.775 --> 00:04:34.763
the same engine inside Microsoft Access.

00:04:34.813 --> 00:04:42.863
VB 4.0 in 1995 was the first version that
could compile to 32-bit code for Windows

00:04:42.913 --> 00:04:49.950
95. And that's right around the era
we're talking about today — 1995, 1996.

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:52.422
VB4 was the current version.

00:04:52.472 --> 00:04:57.680
Windows 95 was brand new and everyone
was excited about the Start menu.

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:05.723
VB 5.0 would come in 1997 and VB 6.0 in
1998 — that last one became the version

00:05:05.773 --> 00:05:07.410
people remember most fondly.

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:13.315
But the spirit of the language, the way
you actually thought about problems and

00:05:13.365 --> 00:05:16.690
wrote code? That was
all established by VB4.

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:26.858
So what did it actually look like to set
up a Visual Basic development environment

00:05:26.908 --> 00:05:27.620
in 1995?

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:29.887
You went to a store.

00:05:29.937 --> 00:05:34.613
A physical store — CompUSA, Egghead
Software, maybe a Babbage's in the mall.

00:05:34.663 --> 00:05:36.067
You bought a box.

00:05:36.117 --> 00:05:42.246
Inside the box was either a set of 3.5-inch
floppy disks or, if you were lucky and

00:05:42.296 --> 00:05:44.790
had a CD-ROM drive, a single disc.

00:05:45.290 --> 00:05:50.796
You ran the installer, it unpacked onto
your hard drive, and you had an integrated

00:05:50.846 --> 00:05:55.240
development environment — an IDE —
that looked like a Windows application.

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:58.585
This IDE had a few key pieces.

00:05:58.635 --> 00:06:05.617
On the left, you had a toolbox — a vertical
strip of icons for things like buttons,

00:06:05.667 --> 00:06:08.926
text boxes, labels, picture
boxes, timers, scroll bars.

00:06:08.976 --> 00:06:15.130
In the center was your form — a blank
gray window that you designed visually.

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:20.096
On the right was the properties panel,
where you could set things like the caption

00:06:20.146 --> 00:06:23.106
on a button or the font size of a label.

00:06:23.156 --> 00:06:27.020
And then there was the code window,
where you wrote your actual BASIC.

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:32.508
This was called "RAD" — Rapid Application
Development. The whole point was speed.

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:37.934
You weren't writing code to create
a window. The IDE did that for you.

00:06:37.984 --> 00:06:44.522
You were writing code to respond to events
— a button click, a timer tick, a key

00:06:44.572 --> 00:06:44.910
press.

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:50.587
Now, for today's project, we're not
going to use any of that visual stuff.

00:06:50.637 --> 00:06:56.560
War is a console program — two CPUs playing
cards against each other in a terminal.

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:58.053
No forms, no buttons.

00:06:58.103 --> 00:07:04.027
But I want you to understand that the form
designer was the reason people bought VB.

00:07:04.077 --> 00:07:07.760
The language itself was just
one part of the package.

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:11.170
The drag-and-drop GUI builder
was the killer feature.

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:20.820
Here's a small confession: you cannot
legally install Visual Basic 4.0 today.

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:24.720
Microsoft stopped selling it
decades ago. There's no download.

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:26.453
The license doesn't transfer.

00:07:26.503 --> 00:07:32.953
You could find it on abandonware sites,
but I'm making a YouTube video, so let's

00:07:33.003 --> 00:07:36.420
stay on the right side of copyright law.

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:43.187
What you can do is use VB.NET — the modern
descendant of Visual Basic, which ships

00:07:43.237 --> 00:07:45.950
with every copy of the .NET SDK.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:51.872
When you install .NET, you get a compiler
called vbc — the Visual Basic compiler.

00:07:51.922 --> 00:07:55.030
It's the real thing.
Microsoft still maintains it.

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:57.640
So we create a new console project:

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:03.706
And we get a .vbproj file and a
Program.vb file. That's our canvas.

00:08:03.756 --> 00:08:09.754
Now, the code I'm going to write uses
modern VB.NET syntax, but I'm deliberately

00:08:09.804 --> 00:08:14.506
writing it in the old style.
One module. One Sub Main.

00:08:14.556 --> 00:08:16.234
Structures instead of classes.

00:08:16.284 --> 00:08:19.258
Manual array management
instead of generic collections.

00:08:19.308 --> 00:08:24.010
Select Case instead of pattern matching.
Do While...Loop instead of LINQ.

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:29.715
If you squint at this code, it should
feel like something you'd see in a VB4

00:08:29.765 --> 00:08:30.700
textbook from 1996.

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:35.888
Two important settings in the project file:
Option Explicit On and Option Strict On.

00:08:35.938 --> 00:08:38.935
In classic VB, variables
didn't need to be declared.

00:08:38.985 --> 00:08:45.027
You could just start using a variable called
x and VB would create it for you on the

00:08:45.077 --> 00:08:48.750
spot as a Variant — a type
that could hold anything.

00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:54.654
This was convenient and also the source
of approximately ten million bugs, because

00:08:54.704 --> 00:09:00.528
if you misspelled a variable name, VB
would just silently create a new empty

00:09:00.578 --> 00:09:03.884
variable instead of telling
you something was wrong.

00:09:03.934 --> 00:09:08.080
Option Explicit forces you to declare
everything. We're being responsible.

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:17.545
Before we write any code, let's make sure
we all know how War works, because you

00:09:17.595 --> 00:09:20.366
might not have played
it since you were eight.

00:09:20.416 --> 00:09:22.874
Or maybe you've never played it at all.

00:09:22.924 --> 00:09:27.889
It's a children's card game — no strategy,
no decisions, pure luck — which makes it

00:09:27.939 --> 00:09:31.650
perfect for a computer simulation,
because there are no choices to model.

00:09:32.150 --> 00:09:36.607
Here are the rules: One. Take a
standard 52-card deck and shuffle it.

00:09:36.657 --> 00:09:40.420
Deal it evenly — 26 cards
to each player, face down.

00:09:40.470 --> 00:09:42.500
Neither player looks at their cards.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:47.931
Two. Each round, both players flip
their top card face-up at the same time.

00:09:47.981 --> 00:09:53.624
Whoever played the higher card takes both
cards and puts them at the bottom of their

00:09:53.674 --> 00:09:56.470
pile. Aces are high —
they beat everything.

00:09:56.970 --> 00:09:57.314
Three.

00:09:57.364 --> 00:10:03.617
If both players flip the same rank — two
sevens, two kings, whatever — it's War.

00:10:03.667 --> 00:10:08.345
Each player places three cards face-down,
then flips a fourth card face-up.

00:10:08.395 --> 00:10:13.466
Whoever's face-up card is higher
takes the entire pot — all ten cards.

00:10:13.516 --> 00:10:17.800
If it's another tie, you
do it again. War can chain.

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:23.764
Four. You keep playing until one player
has all 52 cards. That player wins.

00:10:23.814 --> 00:10:28.490
If a player runs out of cards
during a war, they lose.

00:10:28.990 --> 00:10:34.140
That's it. No bluffing, no bidding, no
trump suits. Just flip, compare, collect.

00:10:34.190 --> 00:10:40.540
A game a five-year-old can play — and a
game that maps beautifully onto about 280

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:41.740
lines of code.

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:47.188
Let's start building.

00:10:47.238 --> 00:10:52.510
The first question any card game has to
answer is: how do you represent a card?

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:59.680
In C++ or C#, you'd probably make
a class, or at least a struct.

00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:06.880
In 1995 Visual Basic, you'd use something
called a User-Defined Type — a Type block.

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:09.760
In VB.NET, the equivalent is Structure.

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:15.040
Same idea: a container that groups
related pieces of data together.

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:23.778
A card has two things: a rank and a suit.
The rank is an integer from 2 to 14.

00:11:23.828 --> 00:11:25.959
Two through ten are obvious.

00:11:26.009 --> 00:11:31.629
Eleven is Jack, twelve is Queen,
thirteen is King, and fourteen is Ace.

00:11:31.679 --> 00:11:33.810
Why not one for Ace?

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:41.357
Because in War, Aces are high — they
beat everything — and if Ace is 14, then

00:11:41.407 --> 00:11:45.950
comparing two cards is just comparing
two integers. Higher number wins.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:47.620
No special cases needed.

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:54.630
The suit is a single character — S, H,
D, or C. Spades, Hearts, Diamonds, Clubs.

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:57.090
War doesn't actually care about suits.

00:11:57.140 --> 00:12:00.370
They're there purely so
the output looks nice.

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:06.930
When the program tells you "Player 1 plays
the Ace of Spades," that's the suit doing

00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:11.030
its job. But the game
logic never looks at it.

00:12:11.530 --> 00:12:17.101
Now, let's talk about something that might
look weird if you're coming from C# or

00:12:17.151 --> 00:12:18.600
C++: the keyword Dim.

00:12:19.100 --> 00:12:22.866
Dim is how you declare a
variable in Visual Basic.

00:12:22.916 --> 00:12:28.209
It stands for "Dimension" — as in,
dimension an array, allocate space for this

00:12:28.259 --> 00:12:33.934
thing. That name goes all the way back
to the original 1964 BASIC at Dartmouth.

00:12:33.984 --> 00:12:38.514
In the earliest versions, DIM was
specifically for declaring the dimensions of

00:12:38.564 --> 00:12:42.330
arrays. Over time, it got
repurposed to declare any variable.

00:12:42.830 --> 00:12:49.355
Fifty years later, VB programmers are still
typing Dim and most of them have no idea

00:12:49.405 --> 00:12:51.820
they're invoking an
abbreviation from 1964.

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:56.889
Compare that to C#, where you'd
write int rank; or char suit;.

00:12:56.939 --> 00:13:01.508
In VB, the type comes after
the name: Dim Rank As Integer.

00:13:01.558 --> 00:13:06.512
It reads more like English: "Dimension
a variable called Rank, as an Integer."

00:13:06.562 --> 00:13:12.285
That's very much a deliberate design choice
— BASIC was always supposed to read like

00:13:12.335 --> 00:13:12.670
English.

00:13:17.170 --> 00:13:22.960
Next, we need a deck. Fifty-two cards:
thirteen ranks times four suits.

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:28.123
Two nested For loops. The outer
loop walks through ranks 2 to 14.

00:13:28.173 --> 00:13:31.024
The inner loop walks
through the four suits.

00:13:31.074 --> 00:13:36.462
We drop each card into the array at
the current index and bump the index.

00:13:36.512 --> 00:13:40.450
After both loops finish, we have
a perfectly ordered 52-card deck.

00:13:40.950 --> 00:13:46.359
Let's pause and look at a few
VB-specific things here. First: Sub.

00:13:46.409 --> 00:13:49.088
Not void, not function — Sub.

00:13:49.138 --> 00:13:55.002
Short for "subroutine." In Visual Basic,
there's a hard distinction between a Sub,

00:13:55.052 --> 00:14:00.916
which does something and doesn't return
a value, and a Function, which does

00:14:00.966 --> 00:14:04.100
something and gives you back a result.

00:14:04.600 --> 00:14:10.663
In C-family languages, a function that
returns nothing is just a function with a

00:14:10.713 --> 00:14:15.030
void return type. In VB, it's
a completely different keyword.

00:14:15.530 --> 00:14:18.763
Second: ByRef. See that
in the parameter list?

00:14:18.813 --> 00:14:25.328
In VB, you had to explicitly say whether
a parameter was passed by reference or by

00:14:25.378 --> 00:14:25.738
value.

00:14:25.788 --> 00:14:31.483
ByRef means the subroutine receives a
pointer to the original — changes are visible

00:14:31.533 --> 00:14:35.175
to the caller. ByVal means it gets a copy.

00:14:35.225 --> 00:14:40.510
In classic VB4, the default was ByRef
for everything, which was efficient but

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:40.920
dangerous.

00:14:41.420 --> 00:14:46.810
It meant you could accidentally modify
data the caller didn't expect you to touch.

00:14:47.310 --> 00:14:54.501
Third: look at the For loop syntax. For
Rank = 2 To 14. Then at the end: Next Rank.

00:14:54.551 --> 00:14:59.836
That Next Rank is closing the loop
and telling you what variable is being

00:14:59.886 --> 00:15:00.217
incremented.

00:15:00.267 --> 00:15:07.839
If you're used to C-style for (int i = 0;
i < 14; i++), this is going to look almost

00:15:07.889 --> 00:15:08.220
quaint.

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:14.217
But there's something kind of nice about
it — when you have nested loops, each Next

00:15:14.267 --> 00:15:19.070
names its variable, so you can see
at a glance which loop is ending.

00:15:19.570 --> 00:15:20.480
Now, the shuffle:

00:15:20.980 --> 00:15:26.638
This is a Fisher-Yates shuffle — the gold
standard for producing an unbiased random

00:15:26.688 --> 00:15:27.046
permutation.

00:15:27.096 --> 00:15:33.162
You walk backwards through the array,
and for each position, you swap it with a

00:15:33.212 --> 00:15:36.016
randomly chosen position at or before it.

00:15:36.066 --> 00:15:39.686
Every possible ordering of
the deck is equally likely.

00:15:39.736 --> 00:15:46.210
This is the same algorithm you'd use in
C++ or C# or Python or anything else.

00:15:46.710 --> 00:15:51.847
It's one of those algorithms that's just
correct, and has been since 1938 when

00:15:51.897 --> 00:15:53.700
Fisher and Yates published it.

00:15:54.200 --> 00:16:00.950
The VB-specific thing to notice
here is For i = 51 To 1 Step -1.

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:04.577
That Step -1 is how you count backwards.

00:16:04.627 --> 00:16:08.657
C-family languages just use
i-- in the for-loop update.

00:16:08.707 --> 00:16:14.550
VB makes you spell it out: "step by
negative one." Verbose, but unambiguous.

00:16:15.050 --> 00:16:20.828
Also notice: every single variable is
declared at the top of the subroutine with

00:16:20.878 --> 00:16:26.656
Dim. In classic VB, you couldn't declare
variables in the middle of the code.

00:16:26.706 --> 00:16:28.737
Everything went at the top.

00:16:28.787 --> 00:16:32.900
It's like writing C89 — all
declarations before any statements.

00:16:32.950 --> 00:16:37.479
C# and modern C++ let you declare
variables wherever you want.

00:16:37.529 --> 00:16:39.560
VB was stricter about this.

00:16:44.060 --> 00:16:47.104
Here's where things get interesting
from a data structures perspective.

00:16:47.154 --> 00:16:52.674
Each player has a hand of cards, and that
hand needs to behave like a queue — first

00:16:52.724 --> 00:16:57.316
in, first out. When you win cards,
they go to the bottom of your pile.

00:16:57.366 --> 00:17:00.410
When you play a card,
it comes off the top.

00:17:00.910 --> 00:17:07.117
In C#, you'd use Queue<Card> and call
it a day. In C++, you'd use std::queue.

00:17:07.167 --> 00:17:10.454
In 1995 VB? You didn't
have generic collections.

00:17:10.504 --> 00:17:13.374
You didn't even have a built-in queue.

00:17:13.424 --> 00:17:18.380
You had arrays, and you had
ReDim, and that was about it.

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:20.030
So we fake it:

00:17:20.530 --> 00:17:26.425
The hand is an array of 52 slots —
the maximum possible, since you could

00:17:26.475 --> 00:17:32.371
theoretically hold all the cards — and
a counter that tracks how many slots are

00:17:32.421 --> 00:17:35.938
actually in use. Cards zero
through Count-minus-one are live.

00:17:35.988 --> 00:17:37.920
Everything past Count is garbage.

00:17:38.420 --> 00:17:44.370
Adding a card to the bottom is simple —
drop it at position Count and increment:

00:17:44.870 --> 00:17:47.213
Drawing from the top is more expensive.

00:17:47.263 --> 00:17:52.340
You take the card at position zero, then
shift everything else forward by one slot:

00:17:52.840 --> 00:17:57.555
That shift is O(n) — every card
in the hand moves one position.

00:17:57.605 --> 00:18:03.053
With a proper circular buffer or a
linked list, you could do this in O(1).

00:18:03.103 --> 00:18:04.886
But we're writing 1995 VB.

00:18:04.936 --> 00:18:10.018
Nobody was thinking about algorithmic
complexity when they had 26 cards in a hand.

00:18:10.068 --> 00:18:12.950
Computers were slower,
but data sets were tiny.

00:18:13.450 --> 00:18:18.880
The whole game finishes in a few hundred
to a couple of thousand rounds, and each

00:18:18.930 --> 00:18:20.935
round shifts at most 52 cards.

00:18:20.985 --> 00:18:24.360
A Pentium 75 could handle
this without breaking a sweat.

00:18:24.860 --> 00:18:27.210
Now, look at the Function return syntax:

00:18:27.710 --> 00:18:33.912
This is one of the weirdest things in VB
if you're coming from any other language.

00:18:33.962 --> 00:18:39.383
To return a value from a function,
you assign to the function's own name.

00:18:39.433 --> 00:18:45.245
You don't write return Top; — you write
DrawTopCard = Top, where DrawTopCard is the

00:18:45.295 --> 00:18:47.980
name of the function you're inside of.

00:18:48.480 --> 00:18:53.230
The function name acts as an implicit
local variable that holds the return value.

00:18:53.730 --> 00:18:58.852
This is pure old-school BASIC heritage.
It's been this way since the 1960s.

00:18:58.902 --> 00:19:03.228
VB.NET also supports the Return keyword,
but the assign-to-function-name style is

00:19:03.278 --> 00:19:08.400
the traditional way, and it's what
you would have seen in the 90s.

00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:17.810
The main game loop is where everything
comes together. Let's walk through Sub Main:

00:19:18.310 --> 00:19:24.297
We build a deck, shuffle it, create two
empty hands, and deal — alternating cards,

00:19:24.347 --> 00:19:31.140
one to Player 1, one to Player 2, until all
52 are distributed. Each player gets 26.

00:19:31.640 --> 00:19:32.310
Then the loop:

00:19:32.810 --> 00:19:35.537
Do While...Loop. In C,
that's while (...) { }.

00:19:35.587 --> 00:19:39.549
It keeps going as long as both
players have at least one card.

00:19:39.599 --> 00:19:43.560
When either player hits zero, the
loop ends and we have a winner.

00:19:44.060 --> 00:19:46.623
The MAX_ROUNDS check is a safety valve.

00:19:46.673 --> 00:19:51.850
War is theoretically capable of cycling
forever — the same cards going back and

00:19:51.900 --> 00:19:53.343
forth in a loop.

00:19:53.393 --> 00:19:58.943
In practice, with a random shuffle, games
do end — my recorded runs took anywhere

00:19:58.993 --> 00:20:00.810
from 150 to 2,008 rounds.

00:20:00.860 --> 00:20:06.410
But just in case, we cap it at
20,000 rounds and call it a draw.

00:20:06.910 --> 00:20:10.060
In my testing, I've never
actually hit the cap.

00:20:10.560 --> 00:20:13.198
Notice the string
concatenation operator: &.

00:20:13.248 --> 00:20:20.366
In C# and C++, you use + to glue
strings together. In VB, you use &.

00:20:20.416 --> 00:20:27.534
The reason is that + in VB does different
things depending on the types involved —

00:20:27.584 --> 00:20:33.806
it might add numbers or concatenate strings
— and this ambiguity caused enough bugs

00:20:33.856 --> 00:20:37.390
that Microsoft recommended
always using & for strings.

00:20:37.890 --> 00:20:43.540
It's one of those "we made it too easy
and now we need a rule to prevent the easy

00:20:43.590 --> 00:20:45.040
thing from biting you" situations.

00:20:45.540 --> 00:20:52.210
Also notice line continuation: the
underscore _ at the end of a line means "this

00:20:52.260 --> 00:20:57.586
statement continues on the next line." In
C-family languages, a statement continues

00:20:57.636 --> 00:20:59.826
until you hit a semicolon.

00:20:59.876 --> 00:21:06.994
In VB, every line is its own statement
by default — the underscore is the escape

00:21:07.044 --> 00:21:10.130
hatch when a line gets too long.

00:21:14.630 --> 00:21:19.982
The PlayRound subroutine is the heart
of the game, and it's where the most

00:21:20.032 --> 00:21:21.140
interesting logic lives.

00:21:21.190 --> 00:21:27.314
It's also where the War mechanic — the
thing the game is named after — actually

00:21:27.364 --> 00:21:27.700
happens.

00:21:28.200 --> 00:21:32.926
Four parameters. The round number is
passed ByVal because we just read it.

00:21:32.976 --> 00:21:38.070
The two hands and the war counter are ByRef
because this subroutine modifies them.

00:21:38.570 --> 00:21:43.080
Inside, there's a pot — an array that
collects all the cards at stake this round:

00:21:43.580 --> 00:21:47.654
The pot can theoretically
hold every card in the game.

00:21:47.704 --> 00:21:53.429
In a normal round, it holds two
cards. In a war, it holds ten.

00:21:53.479 --> 00:21:55.491
In a double war, eighteen.

00:21:55.541 --> 00:22:01.265
In theory, wars can chain indefinitely,
though in practice it's extremely rare to go

00:22:01.315 --> 00:22:02.090
past three.

00:22:02.590 --> 00:22:08.754
Then there's an inner Do...Loop — not
a Do While, just Do...Loop, which is an

00:22:08.804 --> 00:22:14.140
infinite loop that we exit with Exit
Sub when someone wins. Each iteration:

00:22:14.640 --> 00:22:17.651
One — check if either
player is out of cards.

00:22:17.701 --> 00:22:21.630
If you can't play, you lose, and
the other player takes the pot.

00:22:22.130 --> 00:22:26.800
Two — both players draw their top
card. Both cards go into the pot.

00:22:27.300 --> 00:22:33.109
Three — compare ranks. If Player 1's
rank is higher, Player 1 wins the pot.

00:22:33.159 --> 00:22:38.968
Exit Sub. If Player 2's rank is higher,
Player 2 wins the pot. Exit Sub.

00:22:39.018 --> 00:22:40.530
If they're equal? War.

00:22:41.030 --> 00:22:46.708
Each player burns up to three cards face-down
— or fewer, if they don't have three

00:22:46.758 --> 00:22:51.720
left. Then the loop goes back to the
top, draws again, and compares again.

00:22:51.770 --> 00:22:55.300
If it's another tie, another
war. The pot keeps growing.

00:22:55.800 --> 00:22:56.870
Notice this line:

00:22:57.370 --> 00:23:03.153
That colon : is VB's statement separator
— it puts two statements on one line.

00:23:03.203 --> 00:23:07.041
It's the equivalent of
putting two lines on one line.

00:23:07.091 --> 00:23:12.485
Frowned upon by style guides, but
convenient for cases like this where the two

00:23:12.535 --> 00:23:14.040
operations are conceptually atomic.

00:23:14.540 --> 00:23:19.722
The GiveCardsToWinner subroutine at the
end is simple — it walks the pot array and

00:23:19.772 --> 00:23:23.210
adds each card to the
bottom of the winner's hand:

00:23:23.710 --> 00:23:27.360
This is what determines the
eventual outcome of the game.

00:23:27.410 --> 00:23:33.280
The order in which won cards get added
to your hand affects what you'll play in

00:23:33.330 --> 00:23:36.240
future rounds, which affects
who wins those rounds.

00:23:36.290 --> 00:23:41.790
War is deterministic after the shuffle —
the outcome is sealed the moment the cards

00:23:41.840 --> 00:23:44.380
are dealt. There's no
randomness during play.

00:23:44.430 --> 00:23:47.340
The only randomness is
in the initial shuffle.

00:23:51.840 --> 00:23:52.430
Let's run it.

00:23:52.930 --> 00:23:58.747
A double war — Ace against Ace, then Queen
against Queen, before an Ace finally beat

00:23:58.797 --> 00:24:01.313
a 2. Eighteen cards in the pot.

00:24:01.363 --> 00:24:06.080
That's a third of the entire deck
changing hands in a single round.

00:24:06.580 --> 00:24:09.853
Every run is different
because the shuffle is random.

00:24:09.903 --> 00:24:14.284
Sometimes it's over in 150 rounds.
Sometimes it takes more than 2,000.

00:24:14.334 --> 00:24:19.453
The number of wars varies too — ten
in one game, sixty-nine in another.

00:24:19.503 --> 00:24:20.930
But it always terminates.

00:24:21.430 --> 00:24:25.414
This is about 280 lines of Visual Basic.

00:24:25.464 --> 00:24:32.473
Not 280 lines of boilerplate and framework
code — 280 lines that actually do

00:24:32.523 --> 00:24:38.020
something. That's the whole game.
Build, shuffle, deal, play, win. Done.

00:24:42.520 --> 00:24:47.771
Rewind to that first run for a
moment — 617 rounds, Player 1 wins.

00:24:47.821 --> 00:24:51.558
We saw the final scoreboard,
but not the final play.

00:24:51.608 --> 00:24:53.830
Here's how the game actually ended:

00:24:54.330 --> 00:25:00.200
Player 2 didn't just lose a round. Player
2 ran out of cards in the middle of a war.

00:25:00.700 --> 00:25:07.276
Going into round 617, Player 2 had exactly
2 cards left — against Player 1's 50.

00:25:07.326 --> 00:25:09.761
Both played a 10: tie, war.

00:25:09.811 --> 00:25:14.316
Normally each player burns three
cards face-down and flips a fourth.

00:25:14.366 --> 00:25:21.770
But Player 2 only had 1 card left after
playing that 10. So what does the code do?

00:25:22.270 --> 00:25:23.663
It doesn't demand three.

00:25:23.713 --> 00:25:28.352
It caps the burn at however many
cards the shorter hand can spare.

00:25:28.402 --> 00:25:34.483
Player 2 had 1, so both players burned 1.
After the burn, Player 2's hand was empty.

00:25:34.533 --> 00:25:40.975
The loop came back to the top, hit the
empty-hand check, and that was it — Player 1

00:25:41.025 --> 00:25:43.500
took the four-card pot and the game.

00:25:44.000 --> 00:25:49.230
And it happened again, in the
opposite direction, purely by chance.

00:25:49.280 --> 00:25:52.110
The longest run — 2,008 rounds:

00:25:52.610 --> 00:25:57.732
Same mechanic. Player 1 walked into
round 2,008 holding 3 cards against 49.

00:25:57.782 --> 00:26:02.506
Both played an 8, leaving
Player 1 with 2. Both burned 2.

00:26:02.556 --> 00:26:07.280
Player 1's hand was empty, and
Player 2 took the six-card pot.

00:26:07.780 --> 00:26:09.940
This is a subtlety worth noticing.

00:26:09.990 --> 00:26:16.200
The rule isn't "you need three cards for
a war or you forfeit." It's "burn what you

00:26:16.250 --> 00:26:22.092
have, and if there's nothing left to flip,
you're done." A player down to two cards

00:26:22.142 --> 00:26:28.352
going into a war still gets a reduced burn
— one card face-down, one card to compete

00:26:28.402 --> 00:26:30.930
with — and can still win it.

00:26:31.430 --> 00:26:36.020
They only lose if that flip ties
again and the well is truly dry.

00:26:36.520 --> 00:26:41.150
Two of our four recorded runs ended
exactly this way. Nobody planned it.

00:26:41.200 --> 00:26:42.230
The shuffle decided.

00:26:46.730 --> 00:26:49.965
Okay, so we've built a
console War simulator.

00:26:50.015 --> 00:26:52.840
In 1995, what would have happened next?

00:26:53.340 --> 00:26:55.547
You would have put a GUI on it.

00:26:55.597 --> 00:26:58.650
This is where Visual Basic's
form designer would have come in.

00:26:59.150 --> 00:27:04.576
You'd open the IDE, draw a form the size
of a card table, drop some PictureBox

00:27:04.626 --> 00:27:09.367
controls on it to represent the card
positions, maybe load bitmap images of playing

00:27:09.417 --> 00:27:14.842
cards from a resource file, and wire up
the game logic to a Timer control that

00:27:14.892 --> 00:27:17.580
advanced the game one round at a time.

00:27:18.080 --> 00:27:21.950
You'd add a "Deal" button, a "Watch"
button, maybe a speed slider.

00:27:22.450 --> 00:27:27.514
And this is how a lot of actual
software got made in the mid-90s.

00:27:27.564 --> 00:27:33.358
People would start with the logic — get
the algorithms working in a console or a

00:27:33.408 --> 00:27:37.377
simple form — and then layer
the visual design on top.

00:27:37.427 --> 00:27:41.760
Visual Basic made that second step dramatically easier than anything else available.

00:27:42.260 --> 00:27:48.077
The shareware scene of the 1990s was full
of card games, board games, and puzzle

00:27:48.127 --> 00:27:50.032
games written in Visual Basic.

00:27:50.082 --> 00:27:54.726
Windows came bundled with Solitaire
and Minesweeper (written in C), but the

00:27:54.776 --> 00:27:59.419
thousands of freeware and shareware
alternatives that flooded BBS boards and early

00:27:59.469 --> 00:28:03.330
web sites? A huge number
of those were VB apps.

00:28:03.830 --> 00:28:09.292
You'd distribute your game by uploading
it to a BBS or a shareware site.

00:28:09.342 --> 00:28:15.592
Users would download it, but they'd also
need the VB runtime — a DLL file called

00:28:15.642 --> 00:28:19.135
something like VBRUN300.DLL that
shipped separately from your program.

00:28:19.185 --> 00:28:23.860
If the user didn't already have it
installed, your game wouldn't launch.

00:28:24.360 --> 00:28:30.116
So a lot of shareware authors would bundle
the runtime with their download, which

00:28:30.166 --> 00:28:33.849
could turn a 200-kilobyte game
into a megabyte-plus download.

00:28:33.899 --> 00:28:40.070
On a 14.4 modem, that's ten minutes or
more of downloading. For a card game.

00:28:40.570 --> 00:28:43.339
But people did it. And the games worked.

00:28:43.389 --> 00:28:49.329
And if you were a teenager in 1996 who
wanted to make your own version of Solitaire

00:28:49.379 --> 00:28:55.318
or Hearts or Spades, Visual Basic was the
fastest path from "I have an idea" to "my

00:28:55.368 --> 00:28:57.080
friends are playing my game."

00:28:57.580 --> 00:29:03.441
This same code — the structures, the shuffle,
the deck management — could be adapted

00:29:03.491 --> 00:29:05.411
for almost any card game.

00:29:05.461 --> 00:29:10.534
Poker hand evaluation is more complex,
but the deck building and shuffling is

00:29:10.584 --> 00:29:13.293
identical. Blackjack
would be simpler than War.

00:29:13.343 --> 00:29:18.416
Bridge or Hearts would need trick-taking
logic, but the card representation is the

00:29:18.466 --> 00:29:18.810
same.

00:29:19.310 --> 00:29:24.780
This 280-line program is a seed that
could grow into a lot of different things.

00:29:29.280 --> 00:29:31.540
Visual Basic didn't exist in a vacuum.

00:29:31.590 --> 00:29:35.830
In 1995, if you wanted to build a
Windows application, you had choices.

00:29:35.880 --> 00:29:39.790
Let's talk about what they
were and why VB won the market.

00:29:40.290 --> 00:29:43.747
Visual C++ 4.0 was Microsoft's
professional-grade tool.

00:29:43.797 --> 00:29:50.762
MFC — the Microsoft Foundation Classes
— gave you a C++ framework for building

00:29:50.812 --> 00:29:56.274
Windows apps. It was powerful, fast,
and absolutely miserable to learn.

00:29:56.324 --> 00:30:03.288
MFC was notorious for its deep class
hierarchies, its reliance on macros, and its

00:30:03.338 --> 00:30:08.800
unforgiving error messages. Professional
developers used it. Game studios used it.

00:30:09.300 --> 00:30:13.410
Nobody starting from scratch would
choose MFC if they had another option.

00:30:13.910 --> 00:30:16.423
Borland Delphi was the dark horse.

00:30:16.473 --> 00:30:22.402
Released in 1995, Delphi was basically
"Visual Basic but for Object Pascal." It had

00:30:22.452 --> 00:30:27.955
the same kind of drag-and-drop form
designer, the same RAD philosophy, but it

00:30:28.005 --> 00:30:34.362
compiled to native code from day one
and its language — Object Pascal — was

00:30:34.412 --> 00:30:39.060
genuinely well-designed. A lot of experienced
programmers preferred Delphi to VB.

00:30:39.560 --> 00:30:44.601
It was faster at runtime, it had a real
object system with inheritance, and

00:30:44.651 --> 00:30:47.510
Borland's component model was
arguably better than VB's.

00:30:48.010 --> 00:30:51.029
Delphi never caught VB
in market share, though.

00:30:51.079 --> 00:30:55.249
VB had Microsoft behind it, and Microsoft
controlled the operating system.

00:30:55.299 --> 00:30:56.784
VB was the default.

00:30:56.834 --> 00:31:02.155
If you were a self-taught programmer or a
small business writing internal tools, you

00:31:02.205 --> 00:31:04.840
used VB because everyone else used VB.

00:31:05.340 --> 00:31:10.657
The ecosystem was bigger — more books, more
tutorials, more third-party components,

00:31:10.707 --> 00:31:15.130
more people on CompuServe forums
who could answer your questions.

00:31:15.630 --> 00:31:18.877
PowerBuilder was huge in the
corporate database world.

00:31:18.927 --> 00:31:24.234
If you were building a line-of-business
application that talked to an Oracle or

00:31:24.284 --> 00:31:26.706
Sybase database,
PowerBuilder was the tool.

00:31:26.756 --> 00:31:31.651
It had a thing called DataWindows that
made building data-entry forms almost

00:31:31.701 --> 00:31:32.475
trivially easy.

00:31:32.525 --> 00:31:37.420
But it was expensive, corporate, and
basically invisible outside of IT departments.

00:31:37.920 --> 00:31:44.156
And then there was Java, which Sun
Microsystems unveiled in 1995 with the slogan

00:31:44.206 --> 00:31:49.545
"Write Once, Run Anywhere" and shipped
as version 1.0 in January 1996.

00:31:49.595 --> 00:31:53.137
Java was the future — everyone knew it.

00:31:53.187 --> 00:32:00.322
But in 1995, Java was brand new, brutally
slow, had almost no libraries, and its GUI

00:32:00.372 --> 00:32:05.710
toolkit — AWT — was so
ugly it could make you cry.

00:32:06.210 --> 00:32:12.160
Java would eventually eat the
world, but not yet. Not in 1995.

00:32:12.660 --> 00:32:17.978
So Visual Basic sat in a sweet spot: easier
than C++, more established than Delphi,

00:32:18.028 --> 00:32:20.842
cheaper than PowerBuilder,
and more practical than Java.

00:32:20.892 --> 00:32:27.284
It wasn't the best tool for any single job,
but it was a good enough tool for almost

00:32:27.334 --> 00:32:27.999
every job.

00:32:28.049 --> 00:32:33.010
And "good enough for almost everything"
is a very powerful position to be in.

00:32:37.510 --> 00:32:43.060
What if you wanted to build this same
card game in 1995, but you were on a Mac?

00:32:43.560 --> 00:32:45.901
First: you weren't using Visual Basic.

00:32:45.951 --> 00:32:49.486
Microsoft never actually shipped
Visual Basic for the Mac.

00:32:49.536 --> 00:32:54.666
They had something called QuickBASIC for
the Apple Macintosh — released in 1988,

00:32:54.716 --> 00:33:00.643
last updated in 1992 — but that was a
plain BASIC compiler, not the visual,

00:33:00.693 --> 00:33:03.830
drag-and-drop environment that
made VB special on Windows.

00:33:04.330 --> 00:33:10.610
And eventually Mac Office got VBA, the
macro language inside Excel and Word, but

00:33:10.660 --> 00:33:14.680
that was for automating spreadsheets,
not building standalone applications.

00:33:15.180 --> 00:33:19.878
On a Mac in 1995, you'd probably
be using Metrowerks CodeWarrior.

00:33:19.928 --> 00:33:25.489
This was the dominant development environment
for the Mac from the mid-90s onward.

00:33:25.539 --> 00:33:32.827
It used C and C++ and compiled for both
the old Motorola 68k chips and the newer

00:33:32.877 --> 00:33:33.690
PowerPC architecture.

00:33:34.190 --> 00:33:40.140
The Mac Toolbox API — the equivalent
of the Windows API — was C-based, and

00:33:40.190 --> 00:33:43.740
CodeWarrior was how most Mac
developers interacted with it.

00:33:44.240 --> 00:33:48.058
Now, here's a misconception I
want to address: Objective-C.

00:33:48.108 --> 00:33:53.645
Some people assume that Mac programming
in the 90s meant Objective-C. It didn't.

00:33:53.695 --> 00:33:59.662
Objective-C was a NeXTSTEP language — it
was the language of Steve Jobs's other

00:33:59.712 --> 00:34:02.670
company, NeXT, and the
NeXTSTEP operating system.

00:34:03.170 --> 00:34:09.450
In 1995, NeXT was a niche software
company — it had stopped making its famous

00:34:09.500 --> 00:34:14.936
workstations two years earlier — selling
its operating system and developer tools to

00:34:14.986 --> 00:34:16.624
universities and Wall Street.

00:34:16.674 --> 00:34:20.000
Regular Mac developers weren't
using Objective-C at all.

00:34:20.500 --> 00:34:25.758
Objective-C wouldn't become a Mac language
until 2001, when Apple released Mac OS X.

00:34:25.808 --> 00:34:30.687
OS X was built on NeXTSTEP's foundation,
and it brought Objective-C with it.

00:34:30.737 --> 00:34:36.373
So if someone asks "would it have been
harder in Objective-C in 1995?" — the

00:34:36.423 --> 00:34:37.890
question doesn't quite apply.

00:34:38.390 --> 00:34:43.100
You wouldn't have been using Objective-C
unless you were on a NeXT workstation, and

00:34:43.150 --> 00:34:47.860
if you were on a NeXT workstation, you
were probably doing more interesting things

00:34:47.910 --> 00:34:49.220
than a card game.

00:34:49.720 --> 00:34:56.045
For a console card game like ours — no
GUI, just logic and text output — the

00:34:56.095 --> 00:34:58.295
difficulty would have
been roughly comparable.

00:34:58.345 --> 00:35:03.920
C is a harder language than BASIC, but
the actual logic of building a deck,

00:35:03.970 --> 00:35:07.670
shuffling, and comparing cards
is the same in any language.

00:35:07.720 --> 00:35:11.795
You'd use structs, arrays, and printf
instead of structures, arrays, and

00:35:11.845 --> 00:35:13.670
Console.WriteLine. The
algorithm doesn't change.

00:35:14.170 --> 00:35:20.761
Where VB had the massive advantage was
in the next step — putting a GUI on it.

00:35:20.811 --> 00:35:25.449
In VB, that was an afternoon of
dragging controls onto a form.

00:35:25.499 --> 00:35:30.919
On the Mac with CodeWarrior, you'd be
writing Carbon or Toolbox API calls, managing

00:35:30.969 --> 00:35:35.607
window records, handling update events,
and doing your own drawing into grafPorts.

00:35:35.657 --> 00:35:37.560
It was not an afternoon.

00:35:38.060 --> 00:35:41.290
It was a week, minimum, for someone
who knew what they were doing.

00:35:41.790 --> 00:35:46.513
That's the real story of Visual Basic's
dominance: not that it could do things other

00:35:46.563 --> 00:35:51.923
tools couldn't, but that it made the common
case — put a window on the screen with

00:35:51.973 --> 00:35:56.060
some buttons and make them do stuff —
dramatically faster than anything else.

00:36:00.560 --> 00:36:04.949
Visual Basic mattered because it democratized
programming. That's not hyperbole.

00:36:04.999 --> 00:36:10.275
Before VB, writing a Windows application
was a professional skill that required

00:36:10.325 --> 00:36:11.163
significant training.

00:36:11.213 --> 00:36:17.821
After VB, a motivated person with a $100
software purchase and a library book could

00:36:17.871 --> 00:36:20.484
build something real in a weekend.

00:36:20.534 --> 00:36:23.591
Accountants wrote tools to
automate their spreadsheets.

00:36:23.641 --> 00:36:28.030
Teachers built quiz applications. Small
business owners made inventory trackers.

00:36:28.530 --> 00:36:31.590
None of these people would have
called themselves programmers.

00:36:31.640 --> 00:36:36.429
They were people with problems who discovered
that Visual Basic was good enough to

00:36:36.479 --> 00:36:37.120
solve them.

00:36:37.620 --> 00:36:41.910
The language was readable in a
way that C-based languages aren't.

00:36:41.960 --> 00:36:45.856
If PlayerScore > HighScore Then
reads like an English sentence.

00:36:45.906 --> 00:36:50.196
For Each Item In Collection tells
you exactly what it's doing.

00:36:50.246 --> 00:36:56.115
You didn't need to know what a pointer
was. You didn't need to manage memory.

00:36:56.165 --> 00:37:00.850
You didn't need to understand header files
or linking or preprocessor directives.

00:37:01.350 --> 00:37:03.780
You just wrote what you
meant, and it worked.

00:37:04.280 --> 00:37:10.681
Microsoft estimated that by the late 1990s,
there were more lines of Visual Basic

00:37:10.731 --> 00:37:13.907
code in production than
any other language.

00:37:13.957 --> 00:37:20.358
VB6, released in 1998, became one of the
most beloved development tools ever made.

00:37:20.408 --> 00:37:25.427
There are VB6 applications still running
today in banks, hospitals, manufacturing

00:37:25.477 --> 00:37:27.270
plants, and government offices.

00:37:27.770 --> 00:37:32.889
Software that was written a quarter century
ago, still doing its job, and nobody

00:37:32.939 --> 00:37:37.320
wants to touch it because rewriting
it would be expensive and risky.

00:37:37.820 --> 00:37:42.190
And then Microsoft killed it. Well
— they didn't kill it outright.

00:37:42.240 --> 00:37:47.715
What they did was announce, around 2000,
that Visual Basic would become VB.NET — and

00:37:47.765 --> 00:37:53.608
then ship it in 2002 as a completely new
language that shared the name but was

00:37:53.658 --> 00:37:55.450
fundamentally different under the hood.

00:37:55.950 --> 00:38:03.068
VB.NET ran on the .NET framework, used
the Common Language Runtime, and had a

00:38:03.118 --> 00:38:06.140
different syntax, different
semantics, different everything.

00:38:06.190 --> 00:38:12.284
Code from VB6 did not run in
VB.NET. The migration tools were...

00:38:12.334 --> 00:38:13.820
let's say "aspirational."

00:38:14.320 --> 00:38:16.227
The VB community was furious.

00:38:16.277 --> 00:38:22.098
They'd been promised that VB was a platform
they could build careers on, and now

00:38:22.148 --> 00:38:24.837
Microsoft was telling them to start over.

00:38:24.887 --> 00:38:28.360
Many VB6 developers moved to
VB.NET and eventually adapted.

00:38:28.410 --> 00:38:34.622
Many moved to C# instead, figuring that
if they had to learn a new .NET language,

00:38:34.672 --> 00:38:39.710
they might as well learn the one Microsoft
clearly favored. And some just...

00:38:40.210 --> 00:38:43.280
stayed on VB6. Some are still there.

00:38:43.780 --> 00:38:50.584
In 2005, as Microsoft ended mainstream
support for VB6, a group of developers and

00:38:50.634 --> 00:38:55.970
MVPs organized an actual petition to
keep unmanaged Visual Basic alive.

00:38:56.470 --> 00:39:02.118
VB.NET still exists today — it's the
language we wrote this card game in.

00:39:02.168 --> 00:39:04.967
But it's a niche within a niche.

00:39:05.017 --> 00:39:10.259
Microsoft has officially stated that they
will not add new language features to

00:39:10.309 --> 00:39:15.550
VB.NET; it will continue to compile
and run, but it's in maintenance mode.

00:39:15.600 --> 00:39:19.620
C# gets all the new toys.
VB.NET gets security patches.

00:39:20.120 --> 00:39:25.790
The language that taught a generation to
program is, for all practical purposes,

00:39:25.840 --> 00:39:26.230
finished.

00:39:26.730 --> 00:39:32.440
But the ideas it pioneered — visual form
designers, event-driven programming, RAD

00:39:32.490 --> 00:39:38.680
tools, components you can drop onto a
surface and configure through properties —

00:39:38.730 --> 00:39:40.600
those ideas won completely.

00:39:40.650 --> 00:39:47.320
Every modern UI framework, from React to
SwiftUI to Flutter, carries DNA that traces

00:39:47.370 --> 00:39:51.640
back to Alan Cooper's prototype
and Microsoft's Visual Basic.

00:39:53.040 --> 00:39:54.495
So that's Visual Basic.

00:39:54.545 --> 00:39:58.633
A language born from the idea that
programming should be accessible.

00:39:58.683 --> 00:40:03.899
A tool that put software development
within reach of people who'd never taken a

00:40:03.949 --> 00:40:05.027
computer science class.

00:40:05.077 --> 00:40:10.670
A product that dominated the 1990s and
then got swept aside by its own successor.

00:40:11.170 --> 00:40:17.200
And in about 280 lines, we used it to
build something that works: a complete card

00:40:17.250 --> 00:40:22.520
game, with real deck management, a proper
shuffle algorithm, and a game loop that

00:40:22.570 --> 00:40:27.840
handles every edge case — including wars
that chain three and four levels deep.

00:40:28.340 --> 00:40:32.467
If you want to try it yourself,
the code is straightforward.

00:40:32.517 --> 00:40:38.541
Install the .NET SDK — it's free — run
dotnet new console -lang VB, and start

00:40:38.591 --> 00:40:38.921
typing.

00:40:38.971 --> 00:40:43.857
The language will feel strange if you're
used to curly braces and semicolons.

00:40:43.907 --> 00:40:50.691
But give it a few minutes, and you might
start to see what millions of people saw in

00:40:50.741 --> 00:40:52.210
it thirty years ago.

00:40:52.710 --> 00:40:55.620
It's readable. It's obvious. And it works.

00:40:56.120 --> 00:40:57.125
Thanks for watching.

00:40:57.175 --> 00:41:02.048
If you enjoyed this, consider subscribing
— I've got more of these retro language

00:41:02.098 --> 00:41:02.752
deep-dives planned.

00:41:02.802 --> 00:41:08.730
And if you were around in 1995 and you
actually wrote VB4 code, leave a comment and

00:41:08.780 --> 00:41:12.950
tell me what I got wrong.
I want to hear about it.

00:41:13.450 --> 00:41:14.760
See you in the next one.

